  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 1201 AM MST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nAreas of fog will likely develop Sunday night into Monday morning as\nan upper level ridge amplifies over the region and high humidity\nremains trapped in the lower boundary layer. Otherwise, Monday\nwill be warmer most areas, with high temperatures near to \nslightly above average across central and western NM. The upper \nlevel ridge will shift east of the area Monday night and give way \nto increasing southwest flow aloft and moisture advection Tuesday \nahead of an approaching upper level trough. The latest model\nconsensus is for the upper level trough to lift northeast from\nAZ/UT and weaken as it moves across CO/NM. However, sufficient\nforcing from the upper level trough combined with above average\nPWATs across our area will result in a solid round of\nprecipitation that will favor western/northern NM and especially \nthe mountains. Snow levels and amounts will be similar to today's\nevent and may require an advisory for the Tusas Mountains. Breezy\nto windy conditions will come with the upper level trough late\nTuesday into Wednesday, especially across northeast NM in closer\nproximity to a deepening lee side trough. Expect a break late\nWedenesday into Thursday, with another potential round of\nprecipitation by Friday as an upper level low progresses east from\nover the Great Basin. However, model spread is still an issue with\nthis system, so forecast confidence for the end of the week is \nrelatively low at this time.",
